── What this says
  A  two-phase commit, one payer                 1 of 6
  B  netting, one institution                    1 of 6
  C  netting, across institutions                2 of 6

  No timings are reported. Every container here runs emulated, so a
  throughput figure from this machine would describe qemu rather than
  the ledger. Survival under contention does not depend on how fast the
  hardware is — it depends on which keys the transactions touch.
  Predicted by the design: A and B touch one key each, so 1 should survive.
  C is spread over 2 institutions writing separate keys, so 2 should.

OK: contending on a single key let at most one transaction through, in both rounds
OK: spreading over 2 institutions let exactly 2 through, as the design predicts

  Note the shape of the result rather than its size. Throughput here is
  whatever qemu allows; what the design claims is that concurrency scales
  with the number of institutions, and that is what these three numbers
  show. Six transactions across two institutions still only gets two
  through, which is the limit the README states: parallelism is ACROSS
  institutions, not within one.

